<Layout title="Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities" lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        <a href="/en/transport/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Transport</a>
        <span>→</span>
        <span>Reference</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500">
        How to use public transit in Chinese cities. Mobile payment, transit cards, bus tips, and general navigation advice.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p>China’s major cities have world-class metro systems. They’re clean, efficient, cheap, and — crucially — easy to use for English-speaking visitors.</p>

      <h3>General Tips for All Cities</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Payment:</strong> You can buy single-trip tickets at machines (most accept cash and Alipay/WeChat Pay). Alternatively, you can scan QR codes directly at gate barriers via Alipay’s Travel section or WeChat’s transit mini-program.</li>
        <li><strong>Signs:</strong> Station names are in Chinese and English. Announcements are bilingual. You won’t get lost.</li>
        <li><strong>Transfers:</strong> Follow the colored lines and English signs. Transfers between lines are clearly marked.</li>
        <li><strong>Apps:</strong> You can use local navigation apps such as Gaode Maps and Baidu Maps to get metro route guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Buses</h3>
      <p>You can take a bus as a handy backup if the metro doesn’t go straight to your destination, since buses cover more remote areas unreachable by the metro.</p>
      <p>Buses are cheaper than the metro, yet they run more slowly and often get caught in heavy traffic during rush hours. You don’t need to worry about language barriers in tourist cities — most buses announce stops in both Chinese and English. Still, pay close attention to the stop alerts to avoid missing your stop. Also, many buses require scanning your transit QR code both when boarding and alighting, so don’t forget to scan again before you get off.</p>

      <h3>Transport Cards</h3>
      <p>Each city has its own transit card (Beijing: Yikatong, Shenzhen: Shenzhentong). These can be bought at metro stations and topped up with mobile pay, and they work seamlessly on both subways and buses. Transit cards offer small discounts compared to single-ride tickets or QR code payments, but they’re not highly recommended for short-term tourists. But many feature unique, creative designs, so you might pick one up as a souvenir if you’d like.</p>

      <h3>Mobile Payment for Transit</h3>
      <p>It’s the most commonly used and convenient method for locals — no need to buy tickets or carry a separate card.</p>
      <p>Both Alipay and WeChat Pay generate universal transit QR codes valid for subways and buses.</p>
      <p>Open Alipay, click "Transport" on the home screen, select your city, and scan at the turnstile. Payment is deducted from your linked card.</p>
      <p>WeChat works similarly: search for the official "Ride Code" mini-program, select your city, activate your transit pass, and link a bank card to start scanning.</p>
      <p><strong>Tip:</strong> Separate QR codes are available for buses and subways, so double-check you’ve selected the right one before boarding.</p>

      <p><a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/">← Back to the complete transport guide</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>